# CLAUDE.md §9's one unforgeable check: every diagnostic is annotated in
# the source that provokes it.
#
# Port of every_diagnostic_is_annotated_in_the_source_that_provokes_it
# (archive/bootstrap-rs/heroes-cli/tests/golden.rs:344), and panel 085 named it first
# among the tests with NO successor whatever — so this is the module that
# milestone exists for.
#
# WHY IT IS WORTH MORE THAN THE EXPECTATION FILES IT DUPLICATES. §9
# forbids `UPDATE_GOLDEN=1` in `check/` and relies on a human reading the
# diff. A regenerator can rewrite `x.expected` — that is what regenerators
# do — but it **cannot invent an annotation in `x.hero`**. Comparing the
# two turns a convention into an invariant: to fake a green run you would
# have to edit the program as well, in the right place, with the right
# code. rustc's rule and rustc's reason, and the retrofit's own evidence
# is that annotating all twenty-eight cases changed no `.expected` file by
# a single byte.
#
# Two forms, and the second exists because the annotation cannot always
# sit on the offending line (rustc has `//~^` and `//~v` for the same
# reason):
#
#   #~ <code>…    a diagnostic with each code is expected ON THIS LINE
#   #~v <code>…   on the NEXT line — `unterminated.hero` needs it because
#                 a trailing comment would land inside the unterminated
#                 string, and `missing-body.hero` because one diagnostic
#                 points past the last line of the file.
#
# `.fixed` files are deliberately not looked at: a repaired program keeps
# the annotations of diagnostics it no longer has, which is harmless — a
# comment provokes nothing — and only the `.hero` cases produce
# diagnostics.

use absence
use cases
use report
use floors
use mark_readers
use shell
use strings

# `unsupported/` is in the sweep because its kind lives INSIDE the
# diagnostic system precisely so that it inherits this invariant (panel
# 020, the historian's decisive row): a report outside `Diagnostic` would
# be the only test class in the repo exempt from §9.
#
# `permissive/` since defect 211 (2026-10-04): the control arm's own form
# (`suite_golden.hero`'s `shape_permissive`), whose marks are what
# `check --permissive` says. Until then its cases were pinned by their
# `.expected` alone, the one golden directory with an expectation this sweep
# did not hold to its source.
constant DIRECTORIES: [str]
    [
        "tests/golden/check"
        "tests/golden/unsupported"
        "tests/golden/permissive"
    ]

# The directories whose marks witness a thesis rule firing: the normal arm's.
# Never the control arm's, which drops every thesis rule by construction
# (`selfhost/diag.hero`'s `is_thesis_rule`), so a thesis code marked there
# would pin the control arm failing to drop it, never the rule firing.
constant NORMAL_ARM: [str]
    [
        "tests/golden/check"
        "tests/golden/unsupported"
    ]

# **The second sweep, and why there had to be one** (author decision
# 2026-08-26, `/decide` answer `5a`). The mechanism above compares a
# case's `.hero` with its `.expected`, so it can only reach cases that
# HAVE an expectation. Measured at M-separate-compilation close: **19
# annotations lived outside it** — 15 across 11 files in
# `tests/golden/fixedbugs/`, which has zero `.expected` for 16 `.hero`,
# and 4 across 3 in `surface-fixtures/`. Both are driven by
# `suite_surface` rows that run the compiler and match stderr, so there
# was no expectation file to compare against and the marks were
# decorations. The failure message below has said so about its own floor
# since it was written; it could not say it about these.
#
# So this half RUNS the compiler and reads what it prints. Three things
# it has to get right, each one measured on the real tree rather than
# assumed:
#
# 1. **Which command provokes the diagnostic.** `check` for a type error,
#    `build` for anything FFI — the probe block only reaches clang on a
#    build. Measured over all fourteen files: check-then-build resolves
#    every one, so the rule is *try check, and if it says nothing, build*.
# 2. **Which file to run.** A case is a DIRECTORY here, not a file: some
#    of these are modules with no `main`, whose diagnostic is provoked by
#    a neighbour. So every `.hero` in the group that has a `main` is run,
#    and their diagnostics are pooled.
# 3. **The comparison carries the FILE.** `externsignature/` is the case
#    that forced it: the annotation is in `bind.hero` and the `main` is in
#    `wrong.hero`, which is the general shape and not an oddity — a
#    diagnostic names the file it lands in. So both sides are
#    `<file>:<line>:<code>` here, where the sweep above needs only
#    `<line>:<code>`.
constant RUN_ROOTS: [str]
    [
        "tests/golden/fixedbugs"
        "tests/golden/surface-fixtures"
    ]

# The floor: how many diagnostics this file pins. `golden.rs` floored at
# 60, which was a number from when there were sixty — a floor at the real
# count is the same rule with the slack taken out.
#
# **181 over the two `.expected` directories, measured 2026-08-18. 207
# with the run-the-compiler half, measured 2026-08-26** — and the number
# was read out of the suite rather than added up by hand: the floor was
# briefly set impossibly high so the failure message would print the true
# count, which is this file's own rule applied to itself.
# **207 on 2026-08-26, 319 on 2026-09-12.** The census that opened
# M-declared-thresholds could not measure this one from outside — the criterion
# sums extracted markers AND diagnostics produced by running the compiler, so no
# grep replicates it — and it was written down as NOT MEASURED rather than
# estimated. `floors.check` measured it from the inside on the first run, which
# is the method this comment already described: put the floor where it cannot
# pass and read the true count out of the failure.
#
# Until 2026-10-04 the total counted a mark this machine could not ask, as
# stepped aside, so that it could be read the same on every machine; then a
# machine asking nothing of the run roots read what one asking all of them
# did. Since defect 246 it counts what was asked, and `floored` below says
# what a machine that steps programs aside owes instead.
#
# **420 until 2026-09-27, 527 from the merge of defects 109, 110 and 115
# onto panel 180's landing**: the 117 bracket shapes and the new goldens of
# the two lanes took the tree past the floor by more than a quarter, and the
# suite said so and printed the count, the rule working as written.
#
# **668 from 2026-09-28**, the same way: defect 129's twelve goldens, which
# annotate 60 diagnostics, took the tree past 527 by more than a quarter, and
# the suite printed 668.
#
# **659 from defect 130's lane, 2026-09-29**: its seven `fixedbugs-130-*`
# cases, whose marks are the statements a failed block used to hide, took
# the count past the quarter, and the suite printed 659.
# **844 from the same lane's second round, 2026-09-29**: two cases of defect
# 130 and six of 131, the tab-margin one alone holding 43 marks, took the
# count past the quarter again, and the suite printed 844.
#
# **662 from defect 133's lane, 2026-09-29**: with its nine
# `fixedbugs-133-*` cases, 53 diagnostics, and one more in `reserved-words`
# in the tree, the suite said the floor was outgrown and printed 662. The two
# lanes met in one merge that day and the higher number stands, since a floor
# moves one way; the merged tree measured 783 against it, inside the quarter.
#
# **819 where the two lanes met, 2026-09-29**: defect 129's lane merged the
# trunk at `0ba7b084`, which carried lane 130's cases, and on the merged tree
# the suite printed 819. Defect 133's lane merged that trunk the same day
# and kept 819, the higher of the two.
#
# **934 where they met again, 2026-09-29**: lane 130 merged the trunk at
# `9b1f1701`, lane 129's cases on it, and on the merged tree the suite
# printed 934.
#
# **1205 where lanes 132 and X2 met the trunk, 2026-09-29**: lane 132's
# eight cases, lane 133's second round and lane X2's three took the count past
# 934 by more than a quarter, and on the merged tree the suite found 1205.
#
# **1261 where lane X1 met the trunk, 2026-09-29**: lane X1's four cases on
# top of lane X2's, and on the merged tree the suite found 1261.
#
# **1317 where lane Y met the trunk, 2026-09-29**: lane Y's five cases on top
# of lanes X1 and X2, and on the merged tree the suite found 1317.
#
# **1652 at lane recovery-b2's batch gate, 2026-09-30**: its seven cases,
# which annotate 93 diagnostics, on top of lane recovery-b1's, took the count
# past 1317 by more than a quarter, and on the merged tree the suite found
# 1652.
#
# **2148 at lane emit's batch gate, 2026-10-01**: its cases for defects 138,
# 144, 145 and 140, which annotate 140 diagnostics (117 of them defect 144's
# refusals), on top of 356 that reached the trunk inside the quarter since
# the last raise, took the count past 1652 by more than a quarter, and on the
# merged tree the suite found 2148.
#
# **2779 at the round's gate, 2026-10-02**: lanes ffi-macro, arm and
# recovery-b7 (and ci-probe, which added no case) merged into one tree, their
# cases for defects 132, 133, 135, 143, 147, 152, 153 and 154 and panel 185's
# R2 on top of what reached the trunk inside the quarter since the last raise,
# took the count past 2148 by more than a quarter, and on the merged tree the
# suite found 2779.
#
# **3581 at batch 9's gate, 2026-10-04**: the cases of batch 8 and batch 9,
# lane b9-recovery's twelve families among them, and `tests/golden/permissive`
# swept since defect 211 (lane b9-annot, which also stopped counting a mark
# stepped aside for a library the machine lacks), took the count past 2779 by
# more than a quarter, and on the merged tree the suite found 3581, none
# stepped aside.
constant LEAST: i64
    3581

# `only` narrows the sweep to the cases whose name holds it (cases.matching);
# a narrowed run asks neither the thesis witnesses, nor the groups, nor the
# floor, which are questions about the whole.
function run_all(scratch: str, compiler: str, only: str) -> report.Report?
    r: report.Report @ report.empty()
    annotated: i64 @ 0
    witnessed: {str: bool} @ {}

    for dir in DIRECTORIES
        found = cases.matching(found: cases.collect(scratch: scratch, dir: dir)?, only: only)

        for item in found
            source = cases.shown(item.source)
            expectation = read_file(item.expected)

            if source.is_err() || expectation.is_err()
                report.keep_failure(
                    @r,
                    label: "annotations/" + item.name,
                    detail: "  cannot read the case or its expectation"
                )
            else
                claimed = sorted(mark_readers.claimed_by(source.must().text))
                reported = sorted(mark_readers.reported_by(expectation.must()))
                annotated @ annotated + reported.len()

                for code in witness_codes(dir: dir, claimed: claimed)
                    witnessed[code] @ true

                if claimed != reported
                    report.keep_failure(
                        @r,
                        label: "annotations/" + item.name,
                        detail: "  " + item.source + ": the annotations and the diagnostics disagree\n    annotated: " + claimed.join(" ") + "\n    reported:  " + reported.join(" ") + "\n  `#~ <code>` marks this line, `#~v <code>` the next one"
                    )
                else
                    report.keep_pass(@r)

    if only != ""
        return ok(r)
    every_thesis_rule_has_a_witness(@r, witnessed: witnessed)
    run = run_roots(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, roots: RUN_ROOTS)?
    floored(@r, expected: annotated, run: run, floor: LEAST)
    return ok(r)

# What the run roots' half counted: the diagnostics its groups pinned, and the
# marks of the programs it stepped aside for a library this machine lacks.
record Asked
    marks: i64
    stepped: i64

# The run roots' half: every group under `roots`, each program in it asked.
function run_roots(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str, roots: [str]) -> Asked?
    marks: i64 @ 0
    stepped: i64 @ 0

    for group in groups(scratch: scratch, roots: roots)?
        marks @ marks + one_group(@r, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, group: group, @stepped)

    return ok(Asked(marks: marks, stepped: stepped))

# The floor, and what a machine that cannot ask owes it.
#
# **A mark stepped aside did not count toward the floor**, the coordinator's
# ruling on defect 246 (2026-10-04): a skip is not a pass. Until then the floor
# read the marks asked plus the marks stepped aside, and the comment above that
# call said a machine skipping everything still went red here; the sum said
# green.
#
# **The ruling alone could not make that sentence true, measured.** On this
# Mac on 2026-10-04 the expectation half pinned 3228 diagnostics and the run
# roots 133, nothing stepped aside, against a floor of 2779: a machine stepping
# aside every program of the run roots would still read 3228, above it. So
# the run roots also answer to the rule the `unsupported` form keeps
# (`suite_golden.hero`): a third of their marks stepped aside is a machine not
# asking them, red, each skip named all the same.
#
# **Revised the same day, at batch 9's Windows leg: the floor reads the walk's
# reach, every mark found, asked or stepped aside.** The floor was raised to
# this Mac's 3581 at batch 9's gate, where nothing is stepped aside; the
# Windows box steps aside the six marks of programs binding headers it lacks,
# read 3575 asked, and the asked-only floor called a walk that reached every
# file one that did not. A floor exists to catch a walk that stops reaching
# the tree, and a mark stepped aside was reached, so it counts as reached and
# never as passed: the third above is what judges the skips, as the
# `unsupported` form's floor is the tree's count, the same on every platform.
function floored(@r: report.Report, expected: i64, run: Asked, floor: i64)
    floors.check(
        @r,
        label: "annotations",
        found: expected + run.marks + run.stepped,
        floor: floor,
        what: "annotated diagnostics found (" + run.stepped.to_str() + " of them stepped aside for a library this machine lacks, which the third below judges)"
    )
    held = run.marks + run.stepped

    if run.stepped > 0 && run.stepped * 3 >= held
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "annotations",
            detail: "  " + run.stepped.to_str() + " of the " + held.to_str() + " marks the run roots hold were stepped aside for a library this machine lacks: this machine is not asking them"
        )

# SWEEP 001'S AUDIT S13, PORTED AT LAST (2026-09-03). The thesis rules —
# the codes `check --permissive` drops, which is what makes it Part 11's
# control arm — are a hand-maintained list in `selfhost/diag.hero`, and a
# hand-maintained list is the fifth root the sweep named: a code MISSING
# from it silently understates the number the thesis is argued from, and
# a code IN it with no golden is a rule nobody can show firing. The Rust
# bootstrap pinned the set (`measure::gate`'s shape: "adding a diagnostic
# turns it red until somebody writes the code down"); the port did not
# bring it, and measured the evening this was written, three of the
# fourteen — `declaration_in_arm`, `declared_twice`, `ufcs_on_mutable` —
# had no case under `check/` at all, only their own module's unit test.
# So: every code the list names is annotated by at least one case this
# suite already reads. The list is read from the source text rather than
# imported, because a `use` cannot climb out of `tests/harness/`
# (`module_path_climbs`), and a copy of the list here would be the second
# hand-maintained list the audit warns about.
constant THESIS_LIST: str
    "selfhost/diag.hero"

function every_thesis_rule_has_a_witness(@r: report.Report, witnessed: {str: bool})
    text = read_file(THESIS_LIST)

    if text.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "annotations/thesis",
            detail: "  cannot read " + THESIS_LIST + ", where the thesis rules are listed"
        )
        return
    listed = thesis_codes(text.must())

    if listed.len() == 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: "annotations/thesis",
            detail: "  " + THESIS_LIST + " has no `is_thesis_rule` list this suite can read — the list moved, and this reader has to follow it"
        )
        return

    for code in listed
        if witnessed[code].is_err()
            report.keep_failure(
                @r,
                label: "annotations/thesis",
                detail: "  `" + code + "` is a thesis rule (`check --permissive` drops it), and no case under " + NORMAL_ARM.join(" or ") + " annotates it — Part 11's control arm counts a rule nobody can show firing"
            )
        else
            report.keep_pass(@r)

# The codes a case's marks witness firing, `claimed` spelling each mark
# `<line>:<code>`: every one under the normal arm's directories, none under the
# control arm's (`NORMAL_ARM` says why).
function witness_codes(dir: str, claimed: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []

    if !holds(names: NORMAL_ARM, name: dir)
        return out

    for mark in claimed
        parts = strings.split_on(text: mark, byte: 58)
        out @ out.push(parts[parts.len() - 1])

    return out

function holds(names: [str], name: str) -> bool
    for one in names
        if one == name
            return true

    return false

# The quoted codes between `function is_thesis_rule` and the loop that
# reads them, in the order written. Anything else on those lines is not a
# code: the list is one string per line, and that is the shape this reads.
function thesis_codes(diag_source: str) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ []
    inside: bool @ false

    for row in strings.lines(diag_source)
        if strings.starts_with(text: row, prefix: "function is_thesis_rule")
            inside @ true
            continue

        if inside && strings.contains(text: row, needle: "for one in codes")
            return out
        trimmed = strings.trimmed(row)

        if inside && trimmed.len() >= 2 && trimmed[0] == 34 && trimmed[trimmed.len() - 1] == 34
            out @ out.push(trimmed.slice(from: 1, to: trimmed.len() - 1))

    return out

# Every directory whose cases carry no `.expected`: under each of `roots`,
# the root itself where it holds a case (`fixedbugs/`, flat), and each of its
# subdirectories (`surface-fixtures/`). Discovered rather than listed,
# because a list of directories is a premise about the tree and premises
# expire in silence (CLAUDE.md §11) — a new fixture directory joins this
# sweep by existing.
function groups(scratch: str, roots: [str]) -> [str]?
    out: [str] @ []

    for root in roots
        holds_cases: bool @ false

        for entry in shell.list_entries(scratch: scratch, dir: root)?
            if strings.ends_with(text: entry, suffix: ".hero")
                holds_cases @ true

        if holds_cases
            out @ out.push(root)

        for sub in shell.list_directories(scratch: scratch, dir: root)?
            out @ out.push(sub)

    return ok(out.sort())

# One group: run every program in it, pool what the compiler said, and
# compare against every annotation in it. Returns how many diagnostics
# were pinned, for the floor; `stepped_aside` gains the marks of each
# program this machine cannot ask.
function one_group(@r: report.Report, scratch: str, compiler: str, group: str, @stepped_aside: i64) -> i64
    label = "annotations/" + group
    files = shell.list_entries(scratch: scratch, dir: group)

    if files.is_err()
        report.keep_failure(@r, label: label, detail: "  cannot list " + group)
        return 0
    paths: [str] @ []
    texts: [str] @ []

    for path in files.must()
        if !strings.ends_with(text: path, suffix: ".hero")
            continue
        text = read_file(path)

        if text.is_err()
            report.keep_failure(@r, label: label, detail: "  cannot read " + path)
            return 0
        paths @ paths.push(path)
        texts @ texts.push(text.must())

    # **A group holding no mark is no case of this sweep**: nothing of it is
    # ever compared, so nothing of it is run, and nothing of it is stepped
    # aside by name. The formatter's fixtures bind `x.h`, a header no machine
    # has, and named sixteen skips *judged where that is* on every run, of
    # programs no machine judges (measured 2026-10-04, the first run of the
    # step-aside below).
    marked: i64 @ 0

    for text in texts
        marked @ marked + mark_readers.claimed_by(text).len()

    if marked == 0
        return 0
    claimed: [str] @ []
    said: str @ ""
    ran: i64 @ 0
    refusals: [str] @ []
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < paths.len()
        path = paths[at]
        marks = mark_readers.claimed_by(texts[at])
        at @ at + 1

        if strings.contains(text: texts[at - 1], needle: "function main")
            asked = provoked(scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler, path: path)

            # The refusal alone says what happened (defect 317): *the
            # compiler never ran on* stood here before it, of a compiler that
            # had run and written bytes that are not UTF-8.
            if asked.is_err()
                report.keep_failure(@r, label: label, detail: "  " + path + ": " + why_not(asked))
                return 0
            out = asked.must()

            # **A program this machine cannot ask steps aside, marks and all,
            # and only where the case and the machine say so** (defect 246).
            # `ffi-a-field-the-header-has-as-an-array.hero` binds `dirent.h`,
            # which Windows does not have, so the compiler answers
            # `ffi_missing_header` where the mark pins `ffi_field_type`: the
            # machine's answer, judged where the header is (the author's *A* of
            # 2026-10-03). Until 2026-10-04 the compiler's words alone made the
            # step-aside, unnamed: a header this machine has, said to be
            # missing, stepped its program aside green; and the guard meant to
            # keep a case whose marks expect the code compared each
            # `<line>:<code>` mark to a bare code, so it never held. Now
            # `absence` asks the case's own marks at the place told, what its
            # program binds, and the machine; the step-aside is a named skip,
            # and a refused one says why beside any disagreement.
            lacks = absence.machine_lacks_among(
                scratch: scratch,
                path: path,
                modules: read_with(path: path, paths: paths, texts: texts),
                expected: "",
                said: out
            )

            if !lacks.is_err()
                stepped_aside @ stepped_aside + marks.len()
                report.keep_skip(@r, label: label, detail: "  " + path + ": " + lacks.must())
                continue
            why = absence.refused(lacks)

            if why.len() > 0
                refusals @ refusals.push(path + ": " + why)
            said @ said + out
            ran @ ran + 1

        for mark in marks
            claimed @ claimed.push(path + ":" + mark)

    if claimed.len() == 0
        return 0

    if ran == 0
        report.keep_failure(
            @r,
            label: label,
            detail: "  " + claimed.len().to_str() + " annotations here and no program with a `main` to provoke them:\n    " + claimed.join("\n    ")
        )
        return 0
    reported = sorted(mark_readers.reported_with_file(said))

    if sorted(claimed) != reported
        detail: str @ "  the annotations and the diagnostics disagree\n    annotated: " + sorted(claimed).join(" ") + "\n    reported:  " + reported.join(" ") + "\n  `#~ <code>` marks this line, `#~v <code>` the next one. This group has no `.expected`, so what it is compared against is what the compiler printed just now."

        for one in refusals
            detail @ detail + "\n  not skipped for a missing library: " + one

        report.keep_failure(@r, label: label, detail: detail)
        return 0
    report.keep_pass(@r)
    return reported.len()

# The sources `absence` reads for what the program at `path` binds: `path`
# first, then each file of its group that holds no `main`, a module some
# program of the group uses. Never another program: `fixedbugs/` is one group
# of thirty-eight programs, each its own, and a header a neighbour binds is
# not one this program does.
function read_with(path: str, paths: [str], texts: [str]) -> [str]
    out: [str] @ [path]
    at: i64 @ 0

    while at < paths.len()
        if paths[at] != path && !strings.contains(text: texts[at], needle: "function main")
            out @ out.push(paths[at])
        at @ at + 1

    return out

# What the compiler says about one program: `check` first, and `build`
# when check is silent, because an FFI diagnostic only reaches clang on a
# build. Measured over all fourteen annotated files at the close.
#
# **IT RETURNS A RESULT BECAUSE SILENCE AND ABSENCE ARE NOT THE SAME THING.**
# It answered `""` for both until 2026-09-21, and on the Windows CI leg that
# day five groups reported `the annotations and the diagnostics disagree` with
# an empty `reported:` line — which reads as *the compiler said nothing about
# this program* and was in fact *the compiler never ran*. `shell.run` refuses a
# non-start now, so the `?` below carries that apart from a compiler that ran
# and had nothing to say.
function provoked(scratch: str, compiler: str, path: str) -> str?
    checked = shell.run_guarded(scratch: scratch, words: [compiler, "check", path])?

    if checked.code == 1
        return ok(checked.err)
    built = shell.run_guarded(scratch: scratch, words: [compiler, "build", path, "-o", scratch + "/annotated.bin"])?

    return ok(built.err)

# Why `provoked` had nothing to hand back, "" where it had. The shape
# `suite_units.hero`'s `why` uses, over this module's own result type.
function why_not(got: str?) -> str
    return match got
        .ok _  => ""
        .err e => e.msg

# Both sides are compared as sorted `<line>:<code>` strings: Heroes sorts
# numbers, strings and bools and not records (spec § 11 Built-ins), and the pair is
# what the comparison is about. The order is the same on both sides, which
# is all a set comparison needs.
function sorted(marks: [str]) -> [str]
    return marks.sort()

test "a case whose annotation disagrees with its diagnostic is caught"
    # The whole point: this is what a regenerator cannot fake, because it
    # would have to edit the .hero as well.
    claimed = sorted(mark_readers.claimed_by("a   #~ unused_binding\n"))
    reported = sorted(mark_readers.reported_by("x.hero:1:1: error[unused_variant]: ...\n"))
    assert claimed != reported
    same = sorted(mark_readers.reported_by("x.hero:1:1: error[unused_binding]: ...\n"))
    assert claimed == same

test "the thesis list is read from the source, one quoted code per line, and stops at its loop"
    text = "# prose\nfunction is_thesis_rule(d: Diagnostic) -> bool\n    codes = [\n        \"unused_binding\"\n        \"declared_twice\"\n    ]\n\n    for one in codes\n        if d.code == one\n            return true\n    \"not_a_code\"\n"
    got = thesis_codes(text)
    assert got.len() == 2
    assert got[0] == "unused_binding"
    assert got[1] == "declared_twice"
    assert thesis_codes("function other()\n    return 1\n").len() == 0

test "a thesis rule with no witness is one failure, and the real list is found"
    real = read_file("selfhost/diag.hero")
    assert !real.is_err()
    listed = thesis_codes(real.must())
    assert listed.len() >= 14
    all_seen: {str: bool} @ {}

    for code in listed
        all_seen[code] @ true

    green: report.Report @ report.empty()
    every_thesis_rule_has_a_witness(@green, witnessed: all_seen)
    assert green.failed == 0
    assert green.passed == listed.len()
    one_short: {str: bool} @ all_seen
    one_short @ {}

    for code in listed
        if code != "declared_twice"
            one_short[code] @ true

    red: report.Report @ report.empty()
    every_thesis_rule_has_a_witness(@red, witnessed: one_short)
    assert red.failed == 1

# Defect 211, its last reader (2026-10-04): the control arm's cases are swept
# as every other case with an expectation, each mark held to it, and they
# witness no thesis rule. Until then this sweep never opened the directory, so
# the narrowed run below selected nothing.
test "fixedbugs: the control arm's cases are held to their marks, and witness no thesis rule"
    # Every case so named is judged, counted from the directories themselves
    # rather than from a number written here: four on the day this was
    # written, all of them the control arm's.
    scratch = "build/harness-selftest"
    arm = cases.collect(scratch: scratch, dir: "tests/golden/permissive").must()
    in_arm = cases.matching(found: arm, only: "the-control-arm").len()
    assert in_arm >= 4
    named: i64 @ 0

    for dir in DIRECTORIES
        named @ named + cases.matching(found: cases.collect(scratch: scratch, dir: dir).must(), only: "the-control-arm").len()

    swept = run_all(scratch: scratch, compiler: "", only: "the-control-arm")
    assert !swept.is_err()
    assert swept.must().passed == named
    assert swept.must().passed >= in_arm
    assert swept.must().failed == 0

    # A mark there names what `check --permissive` said; the normal arm's
    # marks are the ones that show a rule firing.
    assert witness_codes(dir: "tests/golden/permissive", claimed: ["3:unused_binding"]).len() == 0
    assert witness_codes(dir: "tests/golden/check", claimed: ["3:unused_binding"]) == ["unused_binding"]

    # **The premise the exclusion rests on, owed its test**
    # (`.claude/rules/module-shape.md`): the control arm drops every thesis
    # rule, so no case of its directory marks one. A case that did would pin
    # the control arm failing to drop it, green in both forms.
    listed = thesis_codes(read_file(THESIS_LIST).must())
    seen: i64 @ 0

    for item in arm
        for mark in mark_readers.claimed_by(read_file(item.source).must())
            parts = strings.split_on(text: mark, byte: 58)
            assert !holds(names: listed, name: parts[parts.len() - 1])
            seen @ seen + 1

    assert seen >= 5

# DEFECT 246 at its last reader, held as a test (2026-10-04). The run roots'
# step-aside asked the compiler's words alone and named nothing, and the guard
# meant to keep a case whose marks expect the code compared each
# `<line>:<code>` mark to a bare code, so it never held. Five groups of one
# program each, a stand-in compiler answering each with its `.said` file
# (`absence.said_stub`): a header no machine has, which the program binds, is
# stepped aside by name; `stdio.h` said to be missing is judged, clang finding
# it; a header the program does not bind is judged; a mark pinning the missing
# header where it is told keeps its case judged, and it holds; an ordinary
# diagnostic is judged and holds; a group holding no mark is not run. Under
# the old rule the first stepped aside unnamed and the next three stepped
# aside too: 1 passed, 0 failed, no skip.
test "fixedbugs: a run-root program steps aside only where its case and the machine say so, by name"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = "build/harness-selftest"
    root = scratch + "/annotations-246"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(root).is_err()
    nowhere = absence.NOWHERE
    marked = "  #~ ffi_return_type"
    told = " — clang looked and did not find it"
    lacks = root + "/a-lacks/main.hero"
    assert !plant(path: lacks, source: program(header: nowhere, on_head: "", on_function: marked), said: absence.missing_header_said(header: nowhere, tail: told, at: lacks + ":1:1")).is_err()
    has = root + "/b-has/main.hero"
    assert !plant(path: has, source: program(header: "stdio.h", on_head: "", on_function: marked), said: absence.missing_header_said(header: "stdio.h", tail: told, at: has + ":1:1")).is_err()
    unbound = root + "/c-unbound/main.hero"
    assert !plant(path: unbound, source: program(header: "stdio.h", on_head: "", on_function: marked), said: absence.missing_header_said(header: nowhere, tail: told, at: unbound + ":1:1")).is_err()
    pinned = root + "/d-pinned/main.hero"
    assert !plant(path: pinned, source: program(header: nowhere, on_head: "  #~ ffi_missing_header", on_function: ""), said: absence.missing_header_said(header: nowhere, tail: told, at: pinned + ":1:1")).is_err()
    judged = root + "/e-judged/main.hero"
    said = "error[ffi_return_type]: the header says `seven` returns `long`, and this group says `i32`\n  at " + judged + ":2:5\n"
    assert !plant(path: judged, source: program(header: "stdio.h", on_head: "", on_function: marked), said: said).is_err()

    # A group holding no mark is no case here: never run, never named.
    unmarked = root + "/f-unmarked/main.hero"
    assert !plant(path: unmarked, source: program(header: nowhere, on_head: "", on_function: ""), said: absence.missing_header_said(header: nowhere, tail: told, at: unmarked + ":1:1")).is_err()
    compiler = absence.said_stub(scratch)
    assert !compiler.is_err()
    got: report.Report @ report.empty()
    run = run_roots(@got, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler.must(), roots: [root])
    assert !run.is_err()
    assert run.must().marks == 2
    assert run.must().stepped == 1
    assert got.passed == 2
    assert got.failed == 2
    assert got.skips.len() == 1
    assert strings.starts_with(text: got.skips[0], prefix: "SKIP annotations/" + root + "/a-lacks\n")
    assert strings.contains(text: got.skips[0], needle: "which clang does not find")

    # Each judged case says why the compiler's word was not taken.
    assert strings.contains(
        text: got.failures[0],
        needle: "not skipped for a missing library: " + has + ": clang finds `stdio.h`"
    )
    assert strings.contains(text: got.failures[1], needle: "binds no such header")

# The ruling on defect 246's floor (the coordinator, 2026-10-04): a mark
# stepped aside is not a pass. Measured on this Mac the same day, the
# expectation half alone (3228) clears the floor (2779), so a machine stepping
# aside every run-root program is red only by the third `floored` keeps. Revised
# at batch 9's Windows leg the same day: a mark stepped aside counts toward the
# floor as a mark the walk reached, never as a pass, and the third judges it.
test "fixedbugs: a machine stepping every run-root program aside reads red, and a skip counts toward the floor as reached"
    if !shell.can_run_scripts()
        return
    scratch = "build/harness-selftest"
    root = scratch + "/annotations-246-all"
    assert !shell.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for name in ["a", "b"]
        path = root + "/" + name + "/main.hero"
        said = absence.missing_header_said(
            header: absence.NOWHERE,
            tail: " — clang looked and did not find it",
            at: path + ":1:1"
        )
        assert !plant(path: path, source: program(header: absence.NOWHERE, on_head: "", on_function: "  #~ ffi_return_type"), said: said).is_err()

    compiler = absence.said_stub(scratch)
    assert !compiler.is_err()
    got: report.Report @ report.empty()
    run = run_roots(@got, scratch: scratch, compiler: compiler.must(), roots: [root]).must()
    assert run.marks == 0
    assert run.stepped == 2
    assert got.skips.len() == 2
    assert got.failed == 0

    # The expectation half alone holds its floor; the third turns it red.
    all_aside: report.Report @ report.empty()
    floored(@all_aside, expected: 10, run: run, floor: 10)
    assert all_aside.failed == 1
    assert strings.contains(text: all_aside.failures[0], needle: "this machine is not asking them")

    # A floor the marks found miss is red; one they reach is green, the mark
    # stepped aside counted as reached (10 expected, 9 asked, 1 stepped aside).
    short: report.Report @ report.empty()
    floored(@short, expected: 10, run: Asked(marks: 9, stepped: 1), floor: 21)
    assert short.failed == 1
    reached: report.Report @ report.empty()
    floored(@reached, expected: 10, run: Asked(marks: 9, stepped: 1), floor: 20)
    assert reached.failed == 0

    # The Windows box's counts at batch 9's leg: 3575 asked and six run-root
    # marks stepped aside for headers it lacks (its report), against the floor
    # of 3581 the walk reaches whole; the split of the 3575 is this Mac's 133
    # run-root marks less those six. Green, where the asked-only floor read it
    # red.
    box: report.Report @ report.empty()
    floored(@box, expected: 3448, run: Asked(marks: 127, stepped: 6), floor: 3581)
    assert box.failed == 0
    assert box.passed == 1

# A program for the tests above: one group binding `header`, `on_head` after
# its head and `on_function` after its function, each a mark or nothing.
function program(header: str, on_head: str, on_function: str) -> str
    return "extern \"" + header + "\"" + on_head + "\n    function seven() -> i32" + on_function + "\n\nfunction main()\n    print(seven())\n"

# A program at `path`, and beside it what `absence.said_stub` answers for it.
function plant(path: str, source: str, said: str) -> ()?
    shell.mkdir_all(absence.directory_of(path))?
    write_file(path: path, text: source)?
    write_file(path: strings.without_suffix(text: path, suffix: ".hero") + ".said", text: said)?
    return ok()
